From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: add the CPU PLL clock controller (CPU_PLL_CFG)

Mainline 6.18.36 has no CPU clock for this SoC family at all — not for the
A523, not for the A733. This adds one: a driver for the CPU_PLL_CFG block at
0x08870000, which holds the four CPU PLLs and the three glitch-free CPU clock
muxes, and which every later DVFS patch (`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` cpu@N clocks, `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` OPPs) depends
on. Without it the two clusters sit on whatever boot0 left behind — 1014 MHz on
both, register-confirmed on two boards, while the A76 pair is specified to 2002.

What the hardware actually is
-----------------------------

  DCXO 26 MHz -> [ /P ] -> [ x N ] -> [ /(M0*M1) ] -> [ /EXT_DIVP ] --+
                 \_______ CPU_x_PLL_CTRL_REG +0x00 _______/  CLK_REG  |
                                   |                          +0x1C   |
                            lock detector, bit 28                      v
  CPU_SLOW_CLK (= the DCXO) --------------------------------> mux [26:24] -> cores

Four PLLs (UM 5.1.4.1 "PLL Distribution"): CPU_BACK at
+0x0000, CPU_L at +0x1000 (cores 0-5, A55), CPU_B at +0x2000 (cores 6-7, A76),
CPU_DSU at +0x3000 (the DynamIQ cluster, and via +0x301c[9:0] also AXI, GIC and
APB). CPU_L/B/DSU are linear PLLs sharing one register layout; CPU_BACK is a
generic PLL with a different one.

The interesting part is how a CPU changes its own speed without falling over
while executing the very code that reprograms it. Two properties of this block
make it possible, and both are in the UM, not inferred:

  * the mux is glitch-free. The descriptions of all three CLK registers
    (UM 5.1.10.13, 5.1.10.24, 5.1.10.37) state that it switches without
    glitches and may be reconfigured at run time. A running core can be moved between
    two running sources without ever seeing a runt pulse.
  * mux encoding 000 is CPU_SLOW_CLK, and CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL_REG (+0x3034, UM
    5.1.10.42) selects 00 = DCXO / 01 = CLK32K / 10 = CLK16M_RC. At the reset
    default the bypass is the 26 MHz crystal itself: always running, never
    needs to be relocked, and fast enough that a core parked on it keeps
    servicing interrupts.

So a rate change is: park the cores on the DCXO, pull the PLL out of lock and
reprogram it, wait for lock, park the cores back. The core never executes a
single instruction from a PLL that is mid-relock. It just runs at 26 MHz for the
~100 us the PLL needs (20 us of which the UM mandates outright).

Decision 1: relock by muxing away, not by relocking a live PLL
--------------------------------------------------------------

The vendor BSP never switches the mux. It pins it to encoding 011 in probe()
and relocks the running PLL underneath the cores, trusting the linear frequency
modulation ramp to keep every edge legal. This driver does the opposite, and the
argument is from the UM plus register readback, not from taste:

  1. The UM says this mux exists for exactly this, in the register descriptions cited above,
     and it names CPU_SLOW_CLK as the cluster's backup clock source
     (5.1.4.1: the primary source of cores 0-5 is CPU_L_PLL; their backups are
     the 26 MHz oscillator, CPU_BACK_PLL and a 1.2 GHz source).
  2. The ramp the vendor depends on is not armed by the firmware. On the 8 GiB board, under
     our own mainline kernel, CPU_L_PLL_LFM_REG (+0x1018) reads 0x40000001:
     PLL_LFM_EN (bit 31) = 0, PLL_LFM_RSTN = 1, STEP = 1 — the reset defaults.
     Only the vendor *driver* turns LFM on; on the 6 GiB board, which runs a BSP-derived
     kernel, the same register reads 0xc0000100. A mainline driver that relocks
     a live PLL without first replicating the vendor's PAT0/PAT1/LFM setup would
     be doing an unramped frequency jump under eight running cores.
  3. Mainline's own answer for this exact situation is ccu_mux_notifier_register()
     (drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu_mux.c), used by ccu-sun50i-a64.c, ccu-sun8i-h3.c,
     ccu-sun50i-h616.c, ccu-sun20i-d1.c and five more. Doing something else here
     would need a reason, and there isn't one.
  4. On this project, relocking a live PLL is the known root cause of a GPU
     freeze class.

CPU_BACK_PLL is *not* used as the bypass, even though the UM lists it as a
backup source: boot0 leaves it switched off (PLL_EN = 0 in the +0x0000 readback
on the 8 GiB board), and the UM warns that switching a PLL on or off while the
system runs disturbs the other PLLs and can make the system unstable.
The DCXO is free; the BACK PLL would have to be bought.

Because the bypass is only as safe as CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL_REG says it is, probe()
reads that register and refuses to register any clock at all if it is not 00.
Losing cpufreq is survivable; parking eight cores on 32 kHz is not. Both
reference boards read 0x00000000 there. This is also why the reg mapping must
reach 0x3038, not 0x3034 — the register sits *at* 0x3034.

ccu_pll_notifier_register() is deliberately not stacked on top. That helper
gates and ungates a PLL after a rate change to force a relock; here the
gate-off / reprogram / lock / gate-on cycle is already the UM's own sequence
inside set_rate(), and a second gate cycle would land *after* the mux notifier
had restored the mux — i.e. it would manufacture the very output glitch it
exists to prevent.

Decision 2: the two P's are modelled separately, and EXT_DIVP lives inside the
PLL clock
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There are two dividers called P and they are not the same thing:

  PLL_P     CPU_x_PLL_CTRL_REG[19:16], P = field + 1. A *pre*-divider inside the
            loop; it divides the reference before the multiplier and therefore
            moves the VCO.
  EXT_DIVP  CPU_x_PLL_CLK_REG[17:16], 00=/1 01=/2 10=/4. A *post*-divider
            outside the loop, in a different register, sharing that register
            with the mux. The UM names the mux source it produces "CPU_L_PLL/P"
            — the mux never sees the raw PLL output.

Mainline ccu_nkmp assumes N and P share one register and has no p_reg, so it
cannot express this. (The vendor solved that with a private p_reg field, and
then modelled only EXT_DIVP as "p" and PLL_P not at all.) This driver adds a
small local type instead, struct ccu_a733_cpu_pll, which carries the CTRL
register in ccu_common and the CLK register in a separate divp_reg, names both
fields, and keeps PLL_P = M0 = M1 = 1 by construction — set_rate() writes all
three fields on every transition, recalc_rate() reports whatever is really in
them, and probe() warns if the firmware left something else behind (both boards:
all three fields 0).

Keeping M0 = M1 = 1 is not laziness, it removes a genuine ambiguity: the UM
states the 480..2260 MHz window twice, once against the VCO (bit-31 note:
the VCO frequency should lie between 480 MHz and 2.26 GHz,
VCO = InputFreq*N/P) and once against the output (PLL_N note:
"480MHz<=InputFreq*N/P/(M0*M1)<=2260MHz"). Those two only coincide when
M0*M1 == 1. And with a 26 MHz reference, N alone spans 494..2236 MHz in 26 MHz
steps; every extra degree of freedom would only create second encodings of rates
N already reaches, which is how a determine_rate() becomes non-deterministic.

EXT_DIVP is folded into the PLL clock rather than exposed as a ccu_div child,
and that is a correctness requirement, not a simplification. clk_change_rate()
in drivers/clk/clk.c sends POST_RATE_CHANGE for a clock *before* it descends
into that clock's children. A separate divider child would therefore be
reprogrammed only after the mux notifier had already put the cores back on the
PLL — so on every transition that changes EXT_DIVP (i.e. every entry into and
exit from the lowest operating point) the cores would run one intermediate
frequency that no OPP has a voltage for. Writing both registers inside one
set_rate(), while the cores sit on the DCXO, closes that window completely.

Decision 3: the PLL_UPDATE handshake stays private to this driver
------------------------------------------------------------------

N is a "manual update type" parameter: writing CTRL[15:8] does nothing until
PLL_UPDATE (bit 26, R/WAC: write 1 to apply the configuration; the bit clears itself) is set
and has self-cleared (UM 5.1.4.2). Mainline sunxi-ng has no such feature; its
existing CCU_FEATURE_UPDATE_BIT is a hardcoded BIT(27) applied inside the mux
and divider helpers, which is a different bit doing a different job.

A feature flag in the shared library was considered and rejected. What has to
happen around the N write is not "also set a bit" — it is a nine-step procedure
(output gate off, lock detector off, program N/P/M0/M1, program EXT_DIVP in the
second register, lock detector on, update, poll update, poll lock, 20 us, output
gate on). No existing sunxi-ng set_rate() has a shape that one flag could bend
into that, so a shared flag would mean a new shared code path used by exactly
one driver — while putting a new branch into ccu_mult/ccu_nkmp/ccu_div, which
fifteen in-tree SoCs run through. A driver-local clk_ops touches no shared file
and has zero regression surface for any other Allwinner part. If the A523 or a
later sun60i turns out to have the same block, the normal kernel move is to add
the second user first and generalise then; this type is written so that lifting
it into ccu_lpll.c later is a rename.

The set_rate() sequence follows the UM's *enable* ordering for the handshake,
because the UM's own pre-divider-modulation step list is internally
inconsistent: it writes PLL_UPDATE twice (steps 5 and 6) and then waits for
LOCK_STATUS to be cleared, which cannot be a terminal condition. The enable
sequence in the same section is unambiguous — set PLL_UPDATE, wait for
PLL_UPDATE to clear, wait for LOCK, 20 us — so that is what is implemented, with
a single update, which also trivially satisfies the 1 us minimum spacing
between two updates.

PLL_EN and PLL_LDO_EN are never written. The UM advises against enabling or
disabling the PLLs during use and recommends clearing PLL_OUTPUT_GATE in
PLL_CTRL when a clock is not needed. So the
driver's enable/disable ops are PLL_OUTPUT_GATE (bit 27), nothing else.

Mux parents
-----------

The 3-bit mux encoding is sparse and is *not* the same for the DSU as for the
two core clusters (UM 5.1.10.13 / 5.1.10.24 / 5.1.10.37):

  L/B:  000,001,010 CPU_SLOW_CLK · 011 own PLL/P · 100 PERI0PLL_DIV2 · 101 CPU_BACK_PLL
  DSU:  000,001,010 CPU_SLOW_CLK · 011 own PLL/P · 100 CPU0PLL       · 101 PERI0_600M

so it is modelled with a ccu_mux table: {0, 3, 5} for L/B, {0, 3} for the DSU.
Encoding 100 is left out of both — on L/B it is a tap of the peripheral PLL in
the main CCU, which would mean a cross-CCU DT input for a source we never
select. The vendor driver gives all three muxes one shared parent list ending in
"pll-cpu-back", which is simply wrong for the DSU.

The muxes carry CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, and that is load-bearing rather than
decorative: without it clk_mux_determine_rate_flags() walks every parent and
keeps the fastest rate <= the request, so a rate request could silently move the
cores onto the 26 MHz bypass or onto the (disabled) BACK PLL. With it the
request is forwarded to the parent that is actually selected
(clk_core_determine_rate_no_reparent()), i.e. always the cluster's own PLL, and
the only thing that ever moves the mux is the notifier. CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT then
carries the request down from cpu@N, per the contract with patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu`.

All seven clocks are CLK_IS_CRITICAL. Nothing in the consumer path calls
clk_prepare_enable() on them, so without it clk_disable_unused() would close
PLL_OUTPUT_GATE on the PLL the running cores are muxed to.

CPU_BACK_PLL, and a vendor value that is wrong
-----------------------------------------------

CPU_BACK_PLL is modelled read-only (recalc_rate + is_enabled, no set_rate, no
enable/disable) because nothing here routes it anywhere and boot0 leaves it off.
It is modelled at all so that its rate is described truthfully. Its format is
f = InputFreq * N / M1 / P0 with N = PLL_N + 1, P0 = OUTPUT_DIV[22:20] + 1,
M1 = INPUT_DIV[1] + 1 (UM 5.1.10.1). With the 26 MHz DCXO (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`), 0x40104A00
on the 8 GiB board and 0xf8104a00 on the 6 GiB board both decode to 26 * 75 / 1 / 2 = 975 MHz. The
UM's default note is no witness for the input: V0.92 says 975 MHz "When DCXO
is 26MHz", V1.00 says 900 MHz "When DCXO is 24MHz" (PDF p. 305), same fields. The
vendor driver applies the *linear* PLL's raw-N formula to this register and
reports 1924 MHz; clk_summary on a BSP kernel inherits that error. The BIAS
register offset differs too (BACK at +0x10 with PLL_CP = 0x15, the three linear
PLLs at +0x0c with PLL_CP = 0x05) — the first silicon hint that this is a
different block.

Where every value came from
---------------------------

Spec: A733 user manual v0.92.
  5.1.4.1  PLL distribution, per-cluster backup sources
  5.1.4.2  enable / disable / pre-divider-modulation steps,
                                PLL_UPDATE semantics, the 1 us update interval
  5.1.10.1       CPU_BACK_PLL_CTRL_REG fields
  5.1.10.6       CPU_L_PLL_CTRL_REG fields, VCO window, N raw,
                                N legal set 4/5/8/9/10/12-255, default 0x14
  5.1.10.13      CPU_L_PLL_CLK_REG: mux encodings, "glitch-free",
                                EXT_DIVP encodings
  5.1.10.17      CPU_L_PLL_CTRL_STAT_REG (+0x2c), read-only
                                mirror of the configuration actually in effect
  5.1.10.37      CPU_DSU_PLL_CLK_REG, incl. the AXI/GIC/APB fields
  5.1.10.42      CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL_REG @ +0x3034
  the memory map of the A733 user manual               CPU_PLL_CFG base 0x08870000, size 64K

Silicon, register readback, two boards:
  the 8 GiB board (bin 0x05), our own mainline 6.18.36, devmem, dataset of 2026-07-22:
    +0x0000 0x40104A00  BACK: PLL_EN=0, N=75, P0=2, M1=1 -> 975 MHz, off
    +0x1000 0xF8802700  CPU_L   N=0x27=39 -> 1014 MHz
    +0x2000 0xF8802700  CPU_B   N=0x27=39 -> 1014 MHz
    +0x3000 0xF8801E00  CPU_DSU N=0x1E=30 ->  780 MHz
    +0x1018 0x40000001  LFM_EN = 0 (reset default: boot0 does not arm the ramp)
    +0x101c/+0x201c 0x03000000  mux 011, EXT_DIVP /1
    +0x301c 0x03000105          mux 011, EXT_DIVP /1, AXI/GIC/APB all /2 = 390 MHz
  the 6 GiB board (bin 0x04), BSP-derived kernel, sunxi_dump, read today 2026-07-22:
    +0x0000 0xf8104a00  BACK enabled and locked, N=75, P0=2 -> 975 MHz at the
                        26 MHz DCXO, this time with the PLL actually running.
    +0x1000 0xf8802700  CPU_L  1014 MHz     +0x2000 0xf8804d00  CPU_B N=77 -> 2002 MHz
    +0x3000 0xf8801e00  CPU_DSU 780 MHz     +0x301c 0x03000105
    +0x1018 0xc0000100  LFM_EN = 1 — turned on by the vendor driver, not by boot0
    +0x3034 0x00000000  CPU_SLOW_CLK_SEL = 00 = DCXO (the bypass this driver arms)
    0x07090160 0x983FB0F7  XO_CTRL0, DCXO_ST[15:14] = 0b10 = 26 MHz, byte-identical
                        to the 8 GiB board
  On both boards and in every one of the six CTRL readbacks above, PLL_P[19:16],
  PLL_M0[21:20] and PLL_M1[3:0] read 0, i.e. P = M0 = M1 = 1. That is the single
  assumption the rate model rests on, and it is measured, not assumed.

Rate model, checked against the register-derived operating points:
  determine_rate() searches N over the VCO window and EXT_DIVP over {1,2,4},
  preferring /1 on ties. Fed the twelve frequencies the two boards were caught
  running, it reproduces every (N, EXT_DIVP) pair exactly — 416 = 32 with /2
  (26*16 would put the VCO 64 MHz under the floor), then 30/39/46/54/58/62/66/
  69/73/75/77 with /1 for 780..2002. It also lands 1300 MHz on N = 50, the point
  another board was register-caught running.

Vendor BSP ccu-sun60iw2-cpupll.c was read for ideas and used as evidence for
nothing. Three of its statements are contradicted here: the BACK PLL formula
(1924 vs 975 MHz), the DSU mux parent list, and "p" being PLL_P.

Not vendor behaviour, and why it is safe
-----------------------------------------

The reachable set is wider than the vendor ladder: 26 MHz * N for N in 19..86
(494..2236 MHz), the same divided by 2 (247..1118 MHz in 13 MHz steps) and by 4.
Two of those are worth having and cost nothing electrically, because they are
*below* the lowest frequency any bin already runs at 800 mV:

  494 MHz  N=19, EXT_DIVP /1, VCO 494 MHz — legal, 14 MHz above the floor
  247 MHz  N=19, EXT_DIVP /2, VCO 494 MHz — and 247 < 288 MHz is precisely the
           case the UM gives EXT_DIVP for (outputs below 288 MHz)

Both are a lower frequency at a voltage silicon already sustains at 1014 MHz, so
they need no new voltage and no new evidence beyond the register model. Whether
they become OPPs is patch `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse`'s call and needs the bin table; this patch only
makes them expressible. Nothing above 2002 MHz is proposed: 2028..2236 MHz are
arithmetically reachable and inside the VCO window, but there is no voltage
evidence for them in any bin, and the absolute ceiling stands untouched (global
maximum across all nine vendor bins 1050 mV, datasheet absolute maximum for
VDD-CPUB/VDD-CPUL 1.2 V).

Contract with the patches that build on this one
------------------------------------------------

  `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` provides the parent: CLK_RTC_DCXO = 2, registered as "dcxo". This driver
       takes it by clock-name and derives the whole N range from its rate, so a
       19.2 or 24 MHz part needs no code change.
  `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` must add the controller node with reg size >= 0x3038 and point cpu@N at
       CLK_CPU_L / CLK_CPU_B — the muxes, not the raw PLLs. Pointing at a PLL
       would make the mux-based relock impossible.
  `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` owns the OPP table, opp-supported-hw and cpu-supply; `nvmem-cpufreq-a733-speed-bin-from-efuse` owns the bin.

Verified / not verified
------------------------

Verified: builds. drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733-cpu.o compiles clean
(arm64 cross, W=1, no warnings) against 6.18.36 with `clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu` to `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` applied;
CONFIG_SUN60I_A733_CPU_CCU=y after olddefconfig; the built object contains the
compatible "allwinner,sun60i-a733-cpu-ccu" and the clock names pll-cpu-l/b/dsu,
pll-cpu-back, cpu-l/b/dsu. checkpatch clean. The rate search was verified against
the twelve register-derived operating points listed above. dt_binding_check was
NOT run — dtschema is not installed on this host; the YAML parses and follows
allwinner,sun55i-a523-ccu.yaml.

Not verified: nothing in this patch has been executed on silicon. No board runs
this driver yet, and the register writes it performs have deliberately never
been made — writing the CPU PLL block on a running board means reprogramming the
clock under the kernel doing the writing.

The one observable that would falsify it: with `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply` in place, command
cluster L to 1404 MHz and read the block back. CPU_L_PLL_CTRL_REG (0x08871000)
bits[15:8] must be 0x36 (N = 54) *and* CPU_L_PLL_CTRL_STAT_REG (0x0887102c),
the read-only mirror of the configuration actually in effect, must read the same
0x36, with bit 28 (LOCK) set and bit 26 (PLL_UPDATE) clear; CPU_L_PLL_CLK_REG
(0x0887101c) must read bits[26:24] = 0b011 and bits[17:16] = 0b00. If the
PLL_UPDATE handshake is wrong, CTRL and CTRL_STAT disagree — CTRL holds 0x36 and
CTRL_STAT still holds the old N — and no other observable distinguishes
"written" from "applied". If the mux notifier is wrong, the failure is a hang at
the moment of the transition, not a wrong readback. sysfs is not an acceptable
witness for either: on these boards scaling_cur_freq has already been caught
reporting 2002 MHz while the PLL register held N = 32.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-cpu-ccu.h
@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT */
+/*
+ * Clock indices for the Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) CPU PLL CCU (CPU_PLL_CFG,
+ * 0x08870000).
+ *
+ * CLK_PLL_CPU_L/B/DSU are the linear PLLs *after* their EXT_DIVP post-divider,
+ * i.e. exactly the signal the A733 UM calls "CPU_x_PLL/P" where it lists the
+ * sources of the CPU clock mux. CLK_CPU_L/B/DSU are those muxes. A consumer
+ * that wants to set a CPU frequency asks the mux, not the PLL: the mux carries
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT and is what the driver is allowed to park on the DCXO
+ * while the PLL behind it relocks.
+ *
+ * Indices are appended to, never renumbered.
+ */
+#ifndef _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_CPU_CCU_H_
+#define _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_CPU_CCU_H_
+
+#define CLK_PLL_CPU_L		0
+#define CLK_PLL_CPU_B		1
+#define CLK_PLL_CPU_DSU		2
+#define CLK_PLL_CPU_BACK	3
+
+#define CLK_CPU_L		4
+#define CLK_CPU_B		5
+#define CLK_CPU_DSU		6
+
+#endif /* _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_CPU_CCU_H_ */
